"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,562\nThe hippocampus: making new memories\n\n3\n00:00:09,562 --> 00:00:18,123\nIn 1953, a surgeon removed tissue from deep inside the brain of a 27 year old man to stop his seizures.\n\n4\n00:00:18,519 --> 00:00:19,912\nThe seizures eased.\n\n5\n00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:26,139\nBut the operation took away something no one expected: the ability to form new memories.\n\n6\n00:00:26,309 --> 00:00:28,116\nThe man was Henry Molaison,\n\n7\n00:00:28,315 --> 00:00:30,527\nknown in research as H.M.\n\n8\n00:00:30,957 --> 00:00:35,168\nThe surgeon had removed about two thirds of the hippocampus on each side.\n\n9\n00:00:35,707 --> 00:00:36,480\nH.M.\n\n10\n00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:37,974\ncould hold a conversation,\n\n11\n00:00:38,052 --> 00:00:41,164\nand keep a number in mind by repeating it to himself.\n\n12\n00:00:41,576 --> 00:00:42,909\nBut once he was distracted,\n\n13\n00:00:42,929 --> 00:00:43,698\nit was gone.\n\n14\n00:00:44,177 --> 00:00:47,830\nHe met some researchers for decades and never learned their names.\n\n15\n00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,752\nMany older memories survived,\n\n16\n00:00:51,862 --> 00:00:53,707\nincluding much of his childhood.\n\n17\n00:00:54,129 --> 00:00:57,797\nSo the hippocampus is not where memories are stored for good.\n\n18\n00:00:58,166 --> 00:01:00,042\nIt is needed to make new ones.\n\n19\n00:01:01,712 --> 00:01:03,781\nHe could also still learn skills.\n\n20\n00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:07,509\nAsked to trace a star while watching his hand in a mirror,\n\n21\n00:01:07,808 --> 00:01:10,967\nhe made fewer errors over three days of practice,\n\n22\n00:01:11,252 --> 00:01:13,608\neven though each session felt new to him.\n\n23\n00:01:15,278 --> 00:01:17,158\nSo what does the hippocampus do?\n\n24\n00:01:17,575 --> 00:01:20,248\nIt sits deep inside each temporal lobe,\n\n25\n00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:21,542\ncurved like a seahorse,\n\n26\n00:01:21,562 --> 00:01:23,547\nwhich is where its name comes from.\n\n27\n00:01:24,035 --> 00:01:25,380\nWhen you live through an event,\n\n28\n00:01:25,549 --> 00:01:28,200\ndifferent parts of the cortex handle the sights,\n\n29\n00:01:28,416 --> 00:01:30,195\nthe sounds and the words.\n\n30\n00:01:30,610 --> 00:01:33,482\nThe hippocampus links those parts into one memory,\n\n31\n00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:36,754\nlike an index that records where each part is kept.\n\n32\n00:01:38,124 --> 00:01:44,721\nIn 1973, Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo found that in the hippocampus of rabbits,\n\n33\n00:01:45,002 --> 00:01:48,083\na quick burst of signals made connections stronger,\n\n34\n00:01:48,372 --> 00:01:50,628\nand the change lasted for hours.\n\n35\n00:01:51,118 --> 00:01:56,079\nThis long term potentiation is one way connections may store what we learn.\n\n36\n00:01:56,249 --> 00:01:58,209\nOver the following days and nights,\n\n37\n00:01:58,407 --> 00:02:04,575\nespecially during sleep, new memories are replayed and slowly rely less on the hippocampus.\n\n38\n00:02:06,245 --> 00:02:07,827\nWhy it matters for learning.\n\n39\n00:02:08,287 --> 00:02:11,292\nThe hippocampus handles each new fact or event.\n\n40\n00:02:11,688 --> 00:02:14,025\nIt needs your attention to take something in,\n\n41\n00:02:14,201 --> 00:02:16,112\nand time and sleep to settle it.\n\n42\n00:02:16,674 --> 00:02:21,978\nSeveral short sessions on different days give it more chances than one long session.\n\n43\n00:02:24,148 --> 00:02:26,774\nNew facts and events start in the hippocampus.\n\n44\n00:02:27,078 --> 00:02:29,341\nGive it attention, time and sleep."